## Your database is per-checkout

Two git worktrees of this repo do **not** share a database. The name, the published port and
the compose project are derived from the checkout's own path, so a second worktree cannot
drop or migrate the database the first one is testing against:

```bash
mise run wt:info      # what THIS checkout resolved: database, port, compose project
```

| | primary checkout | a linked worktree |
|---|---|---|
| database | `{{ project_slug }}` | `{{ project_slug }}_wt<hash>` |
| host port | `5432` | `5432 + (hash mod 1000)` |
| compose project | `{{ project_slug }}` | `{{ project_slug }}_wt<hash>` |

Your main checkout keeps the plain name and port 5432, so nothing you already have changes.

- `mise run db:guard` refuses to act on a database that is not this checkout's, and runs
  automatically before `dev`, `test` and every migration task. If it refuses, something is
  pointing this checkout at another one's database — that is a real finding.
- **Integration tests never touch it.** `mise run backend:test:integration` starts a
  throwaway PostgreSQL (Testcontainers, needs Docker); the default `mise run test` needs no
  Docker at all.
- **If port 5432 is already taken on your machine**, set `WF_DB_BASE_PORT` in
  `.mise.local.toml` — it is machine-local, which is why it is not a project setting.
- Run `mise trust` once in each new worktree. An untrusted config resolves these values to
  nothing, which silently puts every checkout back on one database.

